WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.494
What an AI-Native Course Platform Has to Prove Before You

2
00:00:03.494 --> 00:00:06.989
Trust It With Learners. An AI-native course platform can make

3
00:00:06.989 --> 00:00:10.483
it easier to assemble a lesson, produce a visual, draft

4
00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:13.977
a worksheet, or offer a conversation around an idea. That

5
00:00:13.977 --> 00:00:17.472
can be genuinely useful. It is not the same thing

6
00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:20.966
as proving that learners will understand the material, that their

7
00:00:20.966 --> 00:00:24.460
data is handled appropriately, that a course can be accessed

8
00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:27.955
by the people it is meant to serve, or that

9
00:00:27.955 --> 00:00:31.449
the work can move with them if the platform changes.

10
00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:34.943
The right question is not “Can this tool build a

11
00:00:34.943 --> 00:00:38.438
course?” The right question is: What kind of learning experience

12
00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:41.932
does this tool actually make possible, under what conditions, and

13
00:00:41.932 --> 00:00:45.426
what evidence supports that answer? Start with the learner’s job

14
00:00:45.426 --> 00:00:48.921
A course is not a pile of generated modules. It

15
00:00:48.921 --> 00:00:52.415
is an agreement about what a learner is trying to

16
00:00:52.415 --> 00:00:55.910
do and how the experience will help them practice, check

17
00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.404
understanding, ask for help, and retain a usable record of

18
00:00:59.404 --> 00:01:02.898
the work. Before comparing platforms, write one sentence: After this

19
00:01:02.898 --> 00:01:06.393
learning experience, the participant should be able to: ____ Then

20
00:01:06.393 --> 00:01:09.887
ask whether the course needs a sequence, a reflection prompt,

21
00:01:09.887 --> 00:01:13.381
a quiz, feedback, a human conversation, a private workspace, an

22
00:01:13.381 --> 00:01:16.876
accessible video player, captions, downloadable material, or a way to

23
00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:20.370
continue when an app changes. If the answer is yes,

24
00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.864
those are requirements to test—not features to assume. Use a

25
00:01:23.864 --> 00:01:27.359
platform evidence card Question What to ask for Learning design

26
00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:30.853
Can a learner follow a clear path, practice, receive feedback,

27
00:01:30.853 --> 00:01:34.347
and understand what comes next? Human role Where does an

28
00:01:34.347 --> 00:01:37.842
instructor, editor, or qualified reviewer remain responsible? Accessibility What is

29
00:01:37.842 --> 00:01:41.336
documented about captions, transcripts, keyboard use, contrast, reading order, downloads,

30
00:01:41.336 --> 00:01:44.830
and assistive technology? Privacy and data What learner, prompt, upload,

31
00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.325
analytics, voice, or chat data is processed? Is it used

32
00:01:48.325 --> 00:01:51.819
for model training? How is it deleted? Rights and ownership

33
00:01:51.819 --> 00:01:55.313
Who owns uploaded source material, created lessons, learner work, media,

34
00:01:55.313 --> 00:01:58.808
and exports? Portability and recovery Can you export course content,

35
00:01:58.808 --> 00:02:02.302
media, learner records, and data? Can you restore a working

36
00:02:02.302 --> 00:02:05.796
version? Terms and support What is the actual price, renewal,

37
00:02:05.796 --> 00:02:09.291
usage limit, support path, refund rule, and service-continuity commitment? A

38
00:02:09.291 --> 00:02:12.785
documented example is not a license to borrow the promise

39
00:02:12.785 --> 00:02:16.279
Harvard Business School describes Foundry as an AI-native workspace for

40
00:02:16.279 --> 00:02:19.774
founders. Its public materials describe an eight-week Bootcamp powered by

41
00:02:19.774 --> 00:02:23.268
Foundry’s AI platform, with stated work on a business model,

42
00:02:23.268 --> 00:02:26.762
market validation, and a pitch deck. HBS also presents AI

43
00:02:26.762 --> 00:02:30.257
mentorship modeled on faculty alongside live sessions with real experts.

44
00:02:30.257 --> 00:02:33.751
That is a useful example of a particular learning design:

45
00:02:33.751 --> 00:02:37.246
a defined learner, a defined duration, a clear applied task,

46
00:02:37.246 --> 00:02:40.740
a combination of AI-supported and human learning, and an institution

47
00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:44.234
stating what the program is meant to do. It is

48
00:02:44.234 --> 00:02:47.729
not evidence that another vendor’s course builder is based on

49
00:02:47.729 --> 00:02:51.223
the same system, licensed by Harvard, endorsed by Harvard, equally

50
00:02:51.223 --> 00:02:54.717
secure, equally accessible, or likely to produce the same learner

51
00:02:54.717 --> 00:02:58.212
experience. A familiar name in a webinar is not a

52
00:02:58.212 --> 00:03:01.706
product architecture. Ask questions before the trial, not after the

53
00:03:01.706 --> 00:03:05.200
upload HBS’s public Foundry FAQ identifies questions people should expect

54
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.695
to ask about a learning platform: whether data is safe

55
00:03:08.695 --> 00:03:12.189
and confidential, whether it will be used to train AI

56
00:03:12.189 --> 00:03:15.683
models, who owns intellectual property, what security controls exist, whether

57
00:03:15.683 --> 00:03:19.178
data is monetized, and whether the platform claims rights to

58
00:03:19.178 --> 00:03:22.672
uploaded or generated materials. Those are not only questions for

59
00:03:22.672 --> 00:03:26.166
a university-scale program. They are good questions whenever a course

60
00:03:26.166 --> 00:03:29.661
platform asks for a creator’s library, a learner’s personal work,

61
00:03:29.661 --> 00:03:33.155
a voice recording, a payment connection, or a member list.

62
00:03:33.155 --> 00:03:36.649
Run a small, content-only proof Before moving a curriculum or

63
00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:40.144
learner community, make a bounded prototype: Use a short, non-sensitive

64
00:03:40.144 --> 00:03:43.638
lesson and a fictional learner profile. Do not connect payment,

65
00:03:43.638 --> 00:03:47.132
email, CRM, calendar, social, or member systems. Test the exact

66
00:03:47.132 --> 00:03:50.627
experience: lesson sequence, captions, downloads, access, feedback, and export. Record

67
00:03:50.627 --> 00:03:54.121
what worked, what was not documented, what could not be

68
00:03:54.121 --> 00:03:57.615
tested, and how to remove the prototype. Decide whether the

69
00:03:57.615 --> 00:04:01.110
platform earned a larger evaluation—not whether its demo was persuasive.

70
00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.604
That approach respects the learner and protects the work. A

71
00:04:04.604 --> 00:04:08.099
platform should make a course easier to deliver without becoming

72
00:04:08.099 --> 00:04:11.593
the only place the course remembers what it is. Sources

73
00:04:11.593 --> 00:04:15.087
and boundaries This is a vendor-neutral evaluation framework, not an

74
00:04:15.087 --> 00:04:18.582
endorsement, comparison, ranking, partnership claim, purchase recommendation, accessibility certification, or

75
00:04:18.582 --> 00:04:22.076
legal/privacy/security assessment. HBS Foundry is cited only as a documented

76
00:04:22.076 --> 00:04:25.570
example from HBS’s public materials. Product capabilities, terms, prices, access,

77
00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:29.065
and policies change; verify current first-party evidence before relying on

78
00:04:29.065 --> 00:04:32.559
it. Harvard Business School Foundry, “Foundry Bootcamp: Your Path to

79
00:04:32.559 --> 00:04:36.053
Success” (accessed October 7, 2026). Harvard Business School Foundry, “Take

80
00:04:36.053 --> 00:04:39.548
Your Venture to the Next Level” (accessed October 7, 2026).

81
00:04:39.548 --> 00:04:43.042
Harvard Business School Foundry, “Frequently Asked Questions” (accessed October 7,

82
00:04:43.042 --> 00:04:46.536
2026). The course is not the app. The course is

83
00:04:46.536 --> 00:04:50.031
the learner’s ability to do something more clearly, with evidence,

84
00:04:50.031 --> 00:04:53.525
practice, and a path that can outlast a product cycle.
